# ClusterIP Service for pantry-bot.
#
# Under Compose, cloudflared reached the bot via Docker's embedded DNS on the
# service name `bot`, over the project's default bridge network. Nothing was
# published to the host. This Service is the direct equivalent: internal only,
# no NodePort, no LoadBalancer, no hostPort.
#
# The resulting in-cluster DNS name is:
#
#     pantry-bot.pantry-bot.svc.cluster.local:3000
#     (short form, used in the tunnel config: http://pantry-bot.pantry-bot.svc:3000)
#
# i.e. <service>.<namespace>.svc — the Service name and the namespace are both
# `pantry-bot`, which reads oddly but is correct.
#
# >>> MANUAL CUTOVER STEP — the tunnel will NOT work until this is done. <<<
# The Cloudflare tunnel's ingress rules are configured OUTSIDE this cluster:
# either in the Cloudflare Zero Trust dashboard (Networks > Tunnels > this
# tunnel > Public Hostnames) for a remotely-managed tunnel, which is what a
# token-run tunnel normally is. The origin URL there currently points at the
# old Docker service name (`http://bot:3000`). It must be changed to
# `http://pantry-bot.pantry-bot.svc:3000`. See README.md.
#
# ClusterIP rather than headless: cloudflared benefits from the stable virtual
# IP, and a headless Service would hand it the pod IP directly, which changes
# on every Keel-driven redeploy.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: pantry-bot
  namespace: pantry-bot
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: pantry-bot
    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: homelab
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: pantry-bot
  ports:
    - name: http
      port: 3000
      targetPort: http
      protocol: TCP
    # WS_PORT from the app config. Exposed because the Compose network made it
    # reachable to sibling containers too; if the tunnel only ever fronts HTTP,
    # this port is harmless (ClusterIP, not reachable off-node). Remove it only
    # after confirming nothing routes WebSocket traffic here.
    - name: ws
      port: 8080
      targetPort: ws
      protocol: TCP
